# DH Workflow Management - Cursor Rules

**Canonical agent skills** live in **`Skills/agent-skills/`** (also available under **`.cursor/skills`**, a symlink to the same tree). Cursor loads skills from `.cursor/skills` automatically when this folder is open.

## Project Context

This workspace contains a DH (DataHub) project for NeoXam. The project includes specifications, implementations, models, and documentation for feature development or enhancement.

## Technology Stack

- **DataHub**: NeoXam DataHub platform with XML-based pack files (.pack)
- **Python**: Scripts for automation and pack file manipulation
- **PowerShell**: Windows automation scripts (for Windows users)
- **Markdown**: Documentation and specifications
- **Business Rules**: DataHub SmartRule language (custom scripting)
- **Mermaid**: For embedding schemas to .md files

## Project Skills

Domain-specific guidance is in `.cursor/skills/` (same as `Skills/agent-skills/`). Apply these when relevant:

| Skill | Use when |
|-------|----------|
| `datahub-pack-creation-modification` | Creating or editing pack files, lists, workflows |
| `datahub-specification-writing` | Writing specs in Specification/ |
| `datahub-business-rules` | Writing SmartRule logic, status management |
| `datahub-pack-installation` | Installing packages via MCP |
| `datahub-object-retrieval` | Fetching objects via getObjectPackage API |
| `datahub-object-search` | Querying DataHub via REST search API, listing objects, retrieving details without getObjectPackage |
| `datahub-data-model` | Understanding data model structure, MxClassDef, MxList |
| `datahub-testing-validation` | Testing packs, validating IMPACT syntax, smoke tests after install |
| `datahub-mcp-project-setup` | Configuring or syncing the DataHub MCP from `.datahub-workspace.json` (`datahub-mcp-dev-{projectName}`) |

**Related skills** (when combining): pack-creation-modification → data-model (for MxClassDef/MxList structure); object-retrieval → pack-creation-modification (retrieved packs inform new packs); business-rules → br-knowledge MCP (optimized IMPACT code); pack-installation → pack-creation-modification (import order, dependencies).

## File Organization

- **Specification/**: Functional specifications and user stories. All specifications produced or updated by the assistant must be in this folder. Use `{projectName}_SPEC_*.md` naming, where `projectName` comes from `workspace.projectName` in `.datahub-workspace.json`. Agents may read/write
- **Implementation/**: Pack files ready for import. Agents may read/write
- **Implementation/Installed/**: Archive folder for successfully installed packages. Agents may read/write
- **Knowledge/Model/**: Data model definitions, class structures (MxClassDef, MxList definitions), schemas. Agents may read/write
- **Knowledge/Model/Objects/**: Retrieved object packages from getObjectPackage — instance data. Filenames: `{ClassName}_{ObjectCode}.pack`. Agents may read/write
- **Implementation/Temp/**: Temporary storage for getObjectPackage. Remove packages already in Knowledge/Model/Objects to avoid duplication. Agents may read/write
- **Knowledge/Model/Templates/**: Example and template packages for reference. Agents may write
- **Scripts/**: All scripts produced by automation (Python, PowerShell, etc.). Agents may read/write
- **Knowledge/Reference/**: Reference documents, presentations and examples. Read-only for agents
- **User Documentation/**: End-user guides. Agents may write
- **Images/**: Diagrams and screenshots. Read-only for agents

## Code Style (General)

### Python Scripts

- Use Python 3 syntax, PEP 8
- Handle file encoding explicitly (UTF-8)
- Use raw strings (r"...") for Windows file paths
- Add error handling with clear messages

### When Writing Python Scripts

- **Place all scripts in `Scripts/`** — Any script produced (Python, PowerShell, etc.) must be saved in the `Scripts/` folder
- Check for existing content before modifications
- Provide clear success/error messages
- Use defensive programming (check for None, empty strings)
- Preserve original file structure when possible

## Domain Knowledge

- Depending on the project domain

## DataHub Object Links

When suggesting the user check an object in DataHub, **always provide a direct clickable link** instead of navigation instructions (e.g. "Administration → Lists → …").

**Format**: `{datahub.url}/datahub/#/{ClassName}/{objectId}`

**Examples** (use markdown `[label](url)` so links are clickable):
- List (MxList): [MxList/234](http://nxdh-docker-product/datahub/#/MxList/234)
- Control (MxDataControl): [MxDataControl/526](http://nxdh-docker-product/datahub/#/MxDataControl/526)
- Security (MxSecurity): [MxSecurity/123](http://nxdh-docker-product/datahub/#/MxSecurity/123)
- Class definition (MxClassDef): [MxClassDef/575](http://nxdh-docker-product/datahub/#/MxClassDef/575) — use the **numeric database ID**, not CLASS_NAME (e.g. MxWorkflow → 575).

Use the `id` from API responses (search, getObjectPackage) or from pack `LOCAL_ID` / comment (e.g. `<!--MxClassDef 575 v11 -->`). Read `datahub.url` from `.datahub-workspace.json`. **Always use markdown link syntax** `[label](url)` so links are clickable.

## Configuration

All configuration values are read from `.datahub-workspace.json`:

- **Project Name**: `{workspace.projectName}` – used for specification naming (`{projectName}_SPEC_*.md`) and elsewhere
- **MCP Server Path**: `{workspace.centralizedFolder}/{workspace.mcpCentralizedFolder}/mcp-datahub-server/`
- **DataHub URL**: `{datahub.url}`
- **Authentication**: Username `{datahub.username}`, Password `{datahub.password}`
